// Protected authoring proposal. Never include this module in the public/student build.
// Choices are authored with the correct response first; the renderer balances positions.
const items = [];
const q = (module, topic, section, title, prompt, choices, rationale, law = null) => items.push({ module, topic, section, title, prompt, choices, rationale, law });

// WI-01: introduction (topic 1), business and market (topic 2).
q(1,1,'1.1','Reserved property rights','A deed conveys a parcel while expressly reserving a specified mineral interest to the grantor. Ignoring any unstated defect in the instrument, why is knowing the physical parcel alone insufficient to describe what the purchaser acquired?', ['The purchaser must also identify the legal rights conveyed and the interest reserved.','Every conveyance of the surface necessarily includes all reserved subsurface interests.','An express reservation changes the geographic location of the parcel.','A reservation means the purchaser receives only a residential lease.'], 'Real property includes legal interests as well as the physical real estate. The instrument’s stated reservation distinguishes the physical parcel from the bundle of rights conveyed.');
q(1,1,'3.1','Cooperative ownership','An offering gives the purchaser shares in the corporation that owns an apartment building and a proprietary lease for one apartment. What ownership arrangement is described?', ['A cooperative.','A condominium unit conveyed by an individual real-estate deed.','A fee-simple subdivision lot.','A tenancy in common in which no occupancy right is allocated.'], 'A cooperative pairs ownership of shares with an occupancy right under a proprietary lease; it differs from receiving a deed to an individually owned condominium unit.');
q(1,1,'3.3','Recurring ownership costs','A buyer has budgeted $1,900 monthly principal and interest, $320 property taxes, $110 insurance, and $170 association dues. Ignoring maintenance and utilities, what monthly ownership budget covers all four stated charges?', ['$2,500.','$2,220.','$2,390.','$2,070.'], 'The stated recurring charges total 1,900 + 320 + 110 + 170 = $2,500. A principal-and-interest quote is not the complete carrying cost.');
q(1,1,'2.3','Immobility and location','Two otherwise comparable houses face different school-access routes and commute times. A purchaser values one location more even though the buildings could be reproduced at equal cost. Which characteristic most directly explains why reproducing the building does not reproduce the whole property?', ['Land is immobile, so the location and its surrounding influences cannot be moved with the building.','All parcels are physically identical when their lot areas match.','Construction cost fixes the price of every nearby sale.','The purchaser acquires no rights outside the interior walls.'], 'Real estate includes an immobile location. Reproducing a structure does not reproduce access, surroundings, or the market’s response to that location.');
q(1,2,'5.4','Market absorption arithmetic','A market report shows 96 comparable active listings and an average of 16 sales per month over the stated observation period. Assuming that sales pace and no new listings, how many months would the current inventory represent?', ['Six months.','Four months.','Eight months.','Twelve months.'], 'Inventory divided by the stated monthly sales rate is 96 / 16 = 6 months. This is a descriptive scenario, not a promise about future absorption.');
q(1,2,'5.3','Business-cycle evidence','An analyst labels a market “expanding” solely because the current median sale price exceeds last year’s. Meanwhile, transaction volume and employment have fallen. What is the strongest evaluation of the analyst’s conclusion?', ['One price statistic is insufficient; the conflicting activity and employment measures also need evaluation.','A higher median price proves expansion regardless of the mix of properties sold.','Falling employment can never affect housing demand.','A decline in sales proves every individual property has lost value.'], 'Market-cycle judgments require multiple indicators and attention to what the statistics measure. Changes in the sales mix can also affect a median without establishing a uniform price change.');
q(1,2,"5.5","Salesperson and firm association","A newly licensed Wisconsin salesperson wants to negotiate brokerage transactions independently while deciding which firm to join. No statutory exception is stated. What does REEB 17.03(4) require?",["Practice only while associated with a firm.","Practice independently if the individual license is active and compensation is waived.","Negotiate independently until the first transaction closes, then choose a firm.","Operate without association after opening a separate real-estate bank account."],"REEB 17.03(4) ties a salesperson’s real-estate practice to association with a firm. Receiving the individual license does not create a separate firm practice.","REEB 17.03(4)");

// WI-02: agency, topic 3.
q(2,3,'7.3','Continuing confidentiality','After a buyer-client closes a purchase and ends the brokerage relationship, a neighbor asks the agent what maximum price the buyer had privately authorized. No disclosure exception or client consent applies. What should the agent do?', ['Keep the former client’s confidential ceiling private.','Disclose it because closing ends the confidentiality duty.','Disclose it only if the neighbor promises not to repeat it.','Publish it as an example of successful negotiation.'], 'Section 452.133(1)(d) continues the confidentiality duty after the transaction or relationship terminates. Closing does not authorize disclosure of private negotiating information.', 'Wis. Stat. 452.133(1)(d)');
q(2,3,"7.1","Services to an unrepresented party","An unrepresented seller asks the buyer’s firm for accurate information about market conditions within the firm’s knowledge. Which response fits the firm’s duty to parties under 452.133(1)(e)?",["Provide accurate information timely, without breaching confidentiality.","Decline unless the seller first becomes a client entitled to the additional agency duties.","Provide selected adverse statistics only, because negotiating advantage overrides accuracy.","Delay until closing because an unrepresented seller has no pre-closing information rights."],"The accurate-market-information duty applies to parties on request. It coexists with confidentiality and is not permission to manipulate a customer’s understanding.","Wis. Stat. 452.133(1)(e)");
q(2,3,"8.4","Loyalty and known material adverse facts","A client orders the firm to conceal a known material adverse fact that the other party neither knows nor can discover through reasonably vigilant observation. Which duty controls the firm’s response?",["Make the required timely written disclosure.","Obey the client's confidentiality instruction because it was received before the disclosure question arose.","Wait for the recipient to request the fact in writing before providing any disclosure.","Give an oral hint and keep written disclosure within the firm's private client file."],"Section 452.133(1)(c) requires the qualifying material adverse fact to be disclosed. The duties to a client do not erase this duty to parties.","Wis. Stat. 452.133(1)(c), (2)(c)");
q(2,3,"9.1","An interest in the acquiring entity","A licensee negotiating a client’s sale also owns an interest in the entity proposing to buy. Under 452.133(3)(b), what must occur before the licensee engages in this self-interested transaction?",["Obtain all parties' prior written consent in the transaction contract.","Give a separate after-closing disclosure to the purchaser entity and retain its receipt.","Obtain only the seller's oral consent, since the buyer entity already knows its owners.","Disclose the interest in the firm's general brochure but omit it from the transaction contract."],"The statute expressly addresses the firm’s, licensee’s, and specified related interests and requires the prior written consent of all parties in the transaction contract.","Wis. Stat. 452.133(3)(b)");
q(2,3,"11.2","Consent to multiple representation","A firm represents both a purchaser and a seller. The seller has consented to multiple representation in writing; the purchaser has not. What is missing before the firm may provide services in that multiple-representation relationship?",["The purchaser-client's written consent.","A signed allocation of duties between the two licensees, in place of the purchaser's consent.","A supervising broker's approval of multiple representation on behalf of both clients.","The purchaser's consent only if the purchaser supplies part of the firm's commission."],"Section 452.134(2) requires written consent from all clients in the transaction, not merely one side or the associated agents.","Wis. Stat. 452.134(2)");
q(2,3,"11.3","Withdrawal of designated-agency consent","Both clients previously consented to designated agency within one firm. Before the next negotiation, one client delivers written withdrawal of that consent. What is the firm’s proper conclusion?",["Stop relying on that consent and reassess the arrangement.","Continue designated agency until both clients jointly sign a replacement agency agreement.","Treat the withdrawal as ineffective unless it is delivered after the current offer expires.","Permit withdrawal only if the designated licensee also consents in writing."],"Section 452.134(3)(a) permits a client to withdraw consent to designated agency at any time by written notice. The firm must address the changed relationship before proceeding.","Wis. Stat. 452.134(3)(a)");
q(2,3,"11.1","Nondesignated multiple representation","In properly consented multiple representation without designated agency, one client asks the firm to develop a strategy that deliberately favors that client over the firm’s other client. Which response fits 452.134(4)?",["Do not place either client's interests ahead of the other's.","Favor the client whose agency agreement was signed first, while keeping the later client's secrets.","Favor the client paying the greater share of the fee, provided that allocation was disclosed.","Assign priority alternately to each client so that neither receives a permanent advantage."],"Without designated agency, the statute does not permit the firm to advantage one client over the other. Compensation or signing order does not change that restriction.","Wis. Stat. 452.134(4)");
q(2,3,"9.2","Subagent’s duties","A firm is acting as a subagent of the seller’s principal firm. The buyer is not that subagent’s client. Which duty statement is accurate?",["All-party duties, but not the principal firm's additional client duties.","Only duties to the principal firm; parties receiving services are not owed the all-party duties.","The complete buyer-client duties as soon as the unrepresented buyer requests information.","A right to prioritize the subagent's fee because only the principal firm owes loyalty."],"Section 452.133(4) distinguishes all-party duties from client duties and prohibits the subagent from putting its own interests ahead of the principal firm’s client.","Wis. Stat. 452.133(4)");
q(2,3,"7.4","Unbiased presentation","An agent presents a lower-priced cash offer and a higher-priced financed offer. The agent prefers the larger fee from the second offer. What presentation best satisfies the all-party duty concerning proposals?",["Explain each offer's advantages and disadvantages objectively.","Present the higher price as necessarily better without analyzing financing or timing risk.","Delay the cash offer until the client has committed to the offer yielding the larger fee.","Explain only price because discussion of financing differences always constitutes legal advice."],"Section 452.133(1)(g) requires objective and unbiased explanation of the advantages and disadvantages of written proposals; the agent’s compensation preference is not the decision rule.","Wis. Stat. 452.133(1)(g)");
q(2,3,"12.3","Negotiable brokerage fees","At a listing consultation, a seller asks whether Wisconsin fixes the firm’s commission at a standard percentage. Which answer reflects the current WB-1 disclosure?",["No; brokerage fees are negotiable.","Yes; the state fixes the percentage, although the parties can negotiate who pays it.","No statutory percentage exists, but the local association sets a binding minimum for members.","Yes; the applicable percentage is fixed by the property's price range and type."],"The current WB-1 states that brokerage fees are not set by law and are negotiable. A firm must not portray its own proposed compensation as a legally mandatory rate.","WB-1, brokerage fees disclosure");

// WI-03: listings, topic 4.
q(3,4,'14.2','Exclusive-agency listing','Under the parties’ stated exclusive-agency listing, the owner retains the right to sell personally without owing the listing fee, but only one firm is engaged. The owner finds the buyer without that firm’s aid. Which feature distinguishes this from an exclusive-right-to-sell listing?', ['The owner’s reserved right to make the owner’s own sale without the listing fee.','The right of unlimited firms to earn fees simultaneously.','A requirement that every buyer first sign with the listing firm.','A statutory minimum commission paid regardless of the agreement.'], 'REEB 16.02 distinguishes exclusive agency from exclusive right to sell by the owner’s retained ability to procure the sale without the commission liability described in an exclusive-right-to-sell arrangement. The actual agreement still controls.', 'REEB 16.02(2), (3)');
q(3,4,'14.1','Current approved form','A firm’s saved residential listing template bears an obsolete approval version; the Board has since mandated a newer WB-1. Which document should the licensee use for a new listing now?', ['The latest approved version applicable to the transaction.','The obsolete template if the client does not notice the version.','Any internet form if its logo resembles the Board’s.','The old version with only its approval date changed.'], 'REEB 16.06(7) requires the latest approved form version. Re-labeling an obsolete document does not update its substantive text.', 'REEB 16.06(7)');
q(3,4,"17.2","Inspection before a listing","A property owner asks the listing agent to sign the listing before the agent has inspected the accessible structures or asked the owner about the property’s condition. What does REEB 24.07(1)(b) require for a property to be listed?",["Inspect and inquire before signing, requesting a written response.","Take the listing first and postpone all inspection and seller inquiry until a buyer writes an offer.","Obtain a seller questionnaire instead of inspecting accessible structures and surroundings.","Require a structural engineer's report before every listing, even without an identified technical issue."],"The listing-licensee provision requires the inspection and seller inquiry before execution; it is not a universal engineering-inspection requirement.","REEB 24.07(1)(b)");
q(3,4,'15.1','Communicating listing terms','A listing’s written inclusion list excludes a rented water-treatment unit, but a draft marketing sheet says “all equipment included.” Which correction is needed before publication?', ['Correct the marketing claim to match the actual exclusion and rental status.','Keep the broader claim because advertisements override the listing.','Promise the buyer that the rental company will transfer ownership for free.','Remove the exclusion from the private file without the seller’s agreement.'], 'Fair and honest service, reasonable skill and care, and the prohibition against misrepresenting property require a truthful inclusion description. An excluded leased item cannot be marketed as owned and included. REEB 24.07(7) is not the basis here: it concerns another licensee or property listed with another licensee.', 'Wis. Stat. 452.133(1)(a), (b); REEB 24.07(2)');
q(3,4,"17.1","Misleading valuation to obtain a listing","To obtain a listing, an agent knowingly promises a selling price far above the market evidence while planning to demand reductions later. Which conduct rule directly addresses that tactic?",["REEB 24.09, misleading values to obtain an agency contract.","REEB 24.07(5), which permits reliance on the agent's own optimistic estimate as a third-party report.","REEB 16.06(7), because an otherwise current form makes any stated pricing promise lawful.","REEB 24.12, which makes all statements about a prospective listing's value confidential."],"The misconduct is knowingly misleading the prospective client about market value to secure the agency contract, not simply expressing a supported pricing opinion.","REEB 24.09");
q(3,4,'17.3','Home-warranty scope','A home-warranty brochure lists a service-call charge, dollar caps, and an exclusion for a particular pre-existing condition. A buyer thinks the warranty guarantees every future repair. What should the agent explain?', ['Coverage is limited by the actual contract, including its charges, caps, and exclusions.','A home warranty makes the seller’s disclosures unnecessary.','A warranty necessarily covers all structural and environmental defects.','The brochure’s limitations disappear when the premium is paid at closing.'], 'A service contract is not an unlimited property guarantee. Its specific coverage, exclusions, and cost-sharing provisions must be read; it does not replace disclosure or inspection.');
q(3,4,"16.2","Protection after listing termination","The listing contains a valid protection provision that may apply to identified buyers after expiration. The seller assumes expiration erases every possible compensation obligation. What must be evaluated before agreeing?",["The clause's conditions, timing, and facts about this buyer.","Only the printed expiration date, because a listing cannot impose any obligation after it expires.","Only whether the buyer first contacted the seller after expiration, regardless of the actual clause.","Whether a new firm is involved, which automatically cancels every prior protection provision."],"Expiration and surviving contractual protection are separate questions. An agent should evaluate the actual terms rather than promise that every obligation vanishes automatically.");

// WI-04: property interests (5), descriptions (6).
q(4,5,'18.1','Police power','A municipality uses its lawful zoning process to separate incompatible industrial and residential uses. Which government power principally describes regulation of land use for public health, safety, and welfare?', ['Police power.','Escheat.','The private power to grant an easement.','The owner’s power to devise property by will.'], 'Zoning is an exercise of governmental regulatory police power. Escheat concerns property passing to the state in specified succession circumstances, not routine zoning.');
q(4,5,'18.3','Life estate and remainder','A conveyance creates a life estate measured by Maya’s life and then gives the property to Rowan. Under that stated arrangement, what interest does Rowan hold while Maya lives?', ['A remainder interest.','A present leasehold from Maya for a fixed term.','Maya’s life estate itself.','A mortgage lien securing Maya’s debt.'], 'The future interest expressly given to the named third person after the measuring life is a remainder. The question states the conveyance’s operation and does not ask the licensee to draft it.');
q(4,5,'19.2','Appurtenant easement','An instrument gives Parcel Cedar a driveway easement over Parcel Birch, for Cedar’s benefit as land rather than for a named individual personally. Which parcel is the servient estate?', ['Parcel Birch, which bears the driveway burden.','Parcel Cedar, because it receives access.','Both parcels solely because they share a boundary.','Neither parcel, because easements cannot burden land.'], 'An appurtenant easement benefits a dominant parcel and burdens a servient parcel. Here Birch bears the burden and Cedar receives the benefit.');
q(4,5,"21.1","Undivided co-ownership","Two purchasers hold stated 60% and 40% undivided interests as tenants in common. Without a separate agreement allocating occupancy, what does “undivided” mean?",["Each fractional interest extends to the whole parcel.","The 60% holder automatically receives exclusive possession of the most valuable 60% of the site.","The parties own separate physical parcels even though no subdivision or partition has occurred.","The 40% holder has a security interest only and does not own an undivided share of the real estate."],"An undivided share is a fractional ownership interest in the whole, not an automatic physical partition of the parcel.");
q(4,5,"22.2","Condominium common elements","A condominium purchaser receives a deed to a unit plus the associated interest in common elements. Which document is especially important for determining whether the exterior balcony is part of the unit, a common element, or a limited common element?",["The declaration and related recorded condominium documents.","The unit's sale price, because balcony ownership is always proportional to the purchase price.","The listing photograph, because an exterior area reached only from one unit is automatically part of that unit.","The lender's appraisal, which conclusively replaces the declaration's common-element classifications."],"Condominium documents define the unit and common-element interests and restrictions. Appearance or exclusive use alone is not a substitute for those documents.");
q(4,6,'23.4','Area conversion','For this problem use 43,560 square feet per acre. A rectangular tract measures 330 feet by 528 feet. What is its area?', ['4 acres.','2 acres.','3 acres.','8 acres.'], '330 × 528 = 174,240 square feet; 174,240 / 43,560 = 4 acres.');
q(4,6,'23.2','Recorded plat description','A deed identifies “Lot 14, Block 3, Meadow Ridge Addition,” with a reference to its recorded plat. Which description method is being used?', ['Lot and block.','Metes and bounds using a sequence of bearings and distances.','A street address as the entire description.','A mortgage amortization schedule.'], 'The identified lot and block refer to the recorded plat. This differs from describing a boundary through a sequence of courses and distances.');
q(4,6,'23.3','Township and range orientation','A government-survey description locates land in Township 6 North, Range 3 West of the identified principal meridian. What does “Range 3 West” locate?', ['The north-south tier of townships west of the principal meridian.','A township row north of the baseline without any east-west location.','A three-foot easement west of the building entrance.','The third lot on a subdivision plat regardless of the meridian.'], 'Range identifies an east-west position relative to the principal meridian, expressed as a north-south tier of townships. Township north or south identifies the row relative to the baseline.');

// WI-05: liens, topic 7.
q(5,7,'24.2','Lien classification','A borrower voluntarily signs a mortgage that encumbers one identified home as security for a loan. How is this lien classified?', ['Voluntary and specific.','Involuntary and general.','Voluntary and general against every asset automatically.','Involuntary and unrelated to debt.'], 'The owner voluntarily creates the mortgage, and it attaches to the specified collateral rather than automatically to every asset.');
q(5,7,"24.3","Subordination","Lender North holds the senior mortgage and signs an effective subordination agreement giving Lender South’s otherwise junior mortgage priority. What has the agreement changed?",["The two liens' relative priority.","The mortgagor's identity, because subordination substitutes a new borrower for the original borrower.","The outstanding principal balances, because a senior lender must forgive debt to become junior.","The secured property's legal description, because priority changes necessarily divide the parcel."],"Subordination changes agreed priority; it is not a conveyance, payoff, or zoning action.");
q(5,7,'25.2','Stated tax-rate calculation','A worksheet gives a taxable assessed value of $275,000 and a rate of $18 per $1,000. Assume no credits, exemptions, or special assessments for this exercise. What tax does the worksheet calculate?', ['$4,950.','$4,500.','$5,400.','$49,500.'], '275,000 / 1,000 × 18 = $4,950. These are hypothetical inputs, not an asserted Wisconsin locality’s current tax bill.');
q(5,7,"24.4","Paid debt and unreleased mortgage","A seller produces a lender’s paid-in-full letter, but the title commitment still requires release of the mortgage of record. What remains to be resolved for closing?",["Satisfy the required record-release or accepted title evidence.","Rely on the seller's paid-in-full letter as automatically removing the mortgage from the public index.","Omit the requirement because a recorded mortgage cannot matter after a debt is paid.","Treat the title commitment as a guarantee that every listed requirement has already been satisfied."],"Payment and clearing the recorded encumbrance are related but separate matters. The commitment’s release requirement must be addressed with the lender and title professionals.");
q(5,7,'26.2','Construction-payment evidence','A closing reviewer asks the seller for records identifying the contractors and suppliers who furnished a completed addition and evidence of their payment. Why do those records matter to the title review?', ['They help evaluate potential construction claims and what payment or release evidence is needed.','They replace every recorded mortgage satisfaction automatically.','They prove that no building permit was ever required for the work.','They establish a legally fixed sale price for the improved property.'], 'Records about those furnishing improvements and their payment support construction-lien review. They do not by themselves substitute for unrelated releases, permits, or valuation evidence.');
q(5,7,"26.3","Lien versus ownership","A title report identifies a judgment lien affecting the seller’s real estate. Does the lien by itself mean the judgment creditor now owns the parcel?",["No; a lien secures an obligation rather than conveying title.","Yes; recording a judgment lien is legally equivalent to delivering a deed to the creditor.","No; therefore the seller can ignore the lien when conveying the title promised by contract.","Yes; the lien creditor automatically replaces the seller as the listing firm's client."],"A lien and title ownership are distinct. The lien still requires appropriate closing and title treatment even though recording it does not itself convey ownership.");

// WI-06: contracts and forms, topic 8.
q(6,8,'29.2','Bilateral promises','In a signed purchase contract the buyer promises to pay and the seller promises to convey, with performance to occur later. Based on the exchange of promises, how is the contract classified?', ['Bilateral.','Unilateral solely because only one parcel is sold.','A deed already delivered.','A gratuitous revocable license to enter.'], 'The exchange of buyer and seller promises is bilateral. Later performance concerns whether it is executory, not whether the promises are bilateral.');
q(6,8,'29.3','Executory status','A binding sale contract has been formed, but neither the price payment nor the required deed delivery has occurred. How is the contract described with respect to performance?', ['Executory.','Fully executed.','Void solely because closing is in the future.','A recorded satisfaction.'], 'An executory contract has performance obligations still outstanding. Binding formation does not mean all performance has already occurred.');
q(6,8,"29.5","Novation","All necessary parties agree that a replacement buyer takes over and the original buyer is released from the original contractual obligation. What distinguishes this stated arrangement from a mere assignment without release?",["An agreed substitution and release: novation.","An assignment that preserves every original obligation despite the parties' stated release.","A delegation that cannot release the original purchaser even with all necessary parties' agreement.","A title conveyance, because substituting a buyer automatically transfers the fee estate."],"The agreed substitution and release distinguish novation from an assignment that by itself need not release the assignor.");
q(6,8,'30.2','Counterproposal','An owner responds to a buyer’s $410,000 offer by proposing $418,000 instead, clearly treating the response as a counteroffer rather than an acceptance. What is the buyer being asked to decide?', ['Whether to accept the counteroffer’s changed terms.','Whether to pay $410,000 under an acceptance the owner has not given.','Whether a changed price is legally impossible in negotiations.','Whether the agent may accept personally in place of the buyer.'], 'A counterproposal changes the proposed bargain and calls for the receiving party’s decision. The stated response is not an unconditional acceptance of the original price.');
q(6,8,'28.1','No bargain from a price inquiry','A potential buyer asks, “Would the owner consider about $360,000?” The agent has no seller authorization to accept anything, and no offer is submitted or accepted. What has this exchange established?', ['A preliminary inquiry, not an accepted purchase contract on the stated facts.','A binding contract because a dollar amount was mentioned.','Authority for the agent to convey the seller’s deed.','An enforceable seller financing commitment.'], 'A price inquiry without the stated elements of offer and acceptance does not itself create the proposed sale contract. The agent cannot supply missing client authority.');
q(6,8,"29.5","Assignment without release","A purchaser assigns contract rights, but the parties do not agree to release the original purchaser from the obligations. Which conclusion avoids confusing assignment with novation?",["The original purchaser's release has not been established.","The original purchaser is always released because assignment and novation have the same effect.","The seller's consent to receive performance automatically releases every previous obligor.","The assignor can never remain liable once the assignee begins performing the agreement."],"Transferring rights does not by itself prove a release of the original obligation. A release or novation must be established separately.");
q(6,8,"28.2","Misrepresentation and genuine assent","A seller knowingly substitutes a false survey to induce the buyer’s agreement. The buyer discovers the substitution and asks the agent to decide the legal remedy. Which response recognizes the contract issue without acting as counsel?",["Preserve the evidence and refer the fraud-remedy question to counsel.","Promise rescission immediately because every misstatement automatically voids every transaction.","State that signature conclusively cures fraud, regardless of the false survey's effect on assent.","Discard the altered survey once the seller supplies an accurate replacement to the file."],"Misrepresentation can affect genuine assent and remedies; the agent should preserve relevant facts and not issue a legal ruling about the remedy.");
q(6,8,"33.3","Applying an express condition","The parties’ written contingency expressly requires the buyer to deliver the specified notice by 5 p.m. on October 9 to exercise the stated termination right. The buyer merely discusses concerns with a friend on October 8. Under these stipulated terms, what is missing?",["Timely delivery of the notice specified in the contingency.","A conversation before the deadline, because private expressions of concern count as delivered notice.","A signed seller amendment, even though the stated contingency permits the buyer's unilateral notice.","A recorded title instrument, because no contract contingency may operate through notice alone."],"The exercise supplies its condition and deadline expressly. A private conversation is not the required delivery of the specified notice; no outside statutory deadline is being inferred.");
q(6,8,'32.1','Form-completion compensation','A licensee completes an approved offer form as an incident of a brokerage transaction and proposes a separate fee specifically for completing that form. What does REEB 16.05(4) say about that separate charge?', ['The licensee may not make a separate charge for completing the forms.','A separate form-completion fee is mandatory.','The separate fee is permissible whenever it is called a legal-services fee.','Only cash form-completion fees are prohibited.'], 'REEB 16.05(4) expressly prohibits a separate charge for completing an approved form; paragraph (3) separately limits use to incidental real-estate practice.', 'REEB 16.05(4)');
q(6,8,'32.1','Legible deletion of printed text','Both parties instruct the licensee to omit a printed provision from an approved form. Under REEB 16.06(3), which treatment of that printed provision is permitted?', ['Cross it out while leaving it legible.','Erase it invisibly and present the altered document as the unmodified standard form.','Delete it from a reproduction so no one can identify the original text.','Refuse solely because the rule prohibits every crossing-out of printed provisions.'], 'REEB 16.06(3) expressly permits crossing out printed provisions to reflect the agreement if the original provision remains legible. This does not authorize unrestricted redrafting or misleading reproduction.', 'REEB 16.06(3)');
q(6,8,"32.1","Explanation versus legal advice","A buyer asks what a printed financing-contingency section generally does and then asks whether a particular disputed notice guarantees victory in court. How should the licensee distinguish the requests?",["Explain the form generally; refer the specific legal conclusion.","Refuse every general explanation because the printed provisions are legally enforceable language.","Guarantee the notice's legal effect if the same approved form was used in another transaction.","Determine the litigation result whenever the buyer acknowledges that the agent is not an attorney."],"REEB 16.05(2) permits a general explanation of approved-form provisions but does not permit advice on legal rights or the legal effect of a specific contract or conveyance.","REEB 16.05(1), (2)");
q(6,8,'32.1','Pre-prepared addendum that changes printed provisions','A pre-prepared addendum is intended to change printed provisions of an approved form. Under REEB 16.06(5), who must prepare that kind of addendum?', ['An attorney identified on the addendum.','Any unlicensed advertiser who supplies attractive formatting.','The buyer’s moving company.','The licensee acting as a separate legal-drafting service.'], 'REEB 16.06(5) requires an identified attorney for this type of pre-prepared addendum and imposes additional restrictions on optional provisions, blanks, and incorporation. It is not unrestricted licensee drafting authority.', 'REEB 16.06(5)');
q(6,8,'32.1','Copies of signed documents','A buyer signs an offer that the licensee has prepared. The agent plans to withhold the buyer’s copy until the transaction closes. What does REEB 15.02(1) require instead?', ['Prompt delivery of an exact and complete copy to the person who signed.','Delivery only if the seller later accepts.','A verbal summary instead of the signed document.','Destruction of the unsigned counterpart as a substitute for providing a copy.'], 'The signatory-copy duty is prompt and concerns an exact, complete copy; it is not postponed automatically to closing.', 'REEB 15.02(1)');
q(6,8,'32.2','Undisclosed side agreement affecting a lender','During negotiations a licensee learns that the parties have kept part of their actual agreement out of the agreement presented to the lender financing the purchase. What does REEB 24.07(4) require?', ['Timely written disclosure of that fact to the lender.','Silence because every lender is presumed to know all side agreements.','Disclosure only to the appraiser after closing.','A private note that is never sent to the lender.'], 'The rule directly addresses knowledge that the entire agreement has not been disclosed to the financing institution or other secured lender. It requires timely written disclosure to that lender.', 'REEB 24.07(4)');
q(6,8,'29.2','Option right','For valid consideration, an owner grants a purchaser the right, but not the obligation, to buy the described property on specified terms during a stated period. What has the purchaser received?', ['An option to purchase.','An immediate duty to purchase regardless of election.','A mortgage satisfaction.','An automatically renewed residential lease.'], 'An option grants the election described in its terms. The option holder is not required to purchase merely because the right exists.');
q(6,8,"32.1","No approved form for the purpose","There is no Board-approved form for the intended real-estate transaction. Under REEB 16.04(2), what non-approved form may the licensee use under the stated exception?",["An identified party- or attorney-drafted form.","Any licensee-created form if the agent labels it a draft and adds a general no-legal-advice disclaimer.","Only an unrelated approved form, regardless of whether its purpose fits the transaction.","A form drafted by anyone, provided the drafter's name is removed before the parties sign."],"REEB 16.04(2) provides the identified party-or-attorney-drafted exception when no Board-approved form is provided for that purpose. It is not a general authority to practice law.","REEB 16.04(2)");

// WI-07: title, topic 9.
q(7,9,"44.3","Commitment requirement","A title commitment lists “obtain a release of Mortgage X” as a condition for issuing the proposed policy. Is that item best read as an already-completed cure?",["No; the release remains an outstanding requirement.","Yes; issuing a commitment represents that every condition listed in its requirements has been met.","No; but the listed requirement automatically disappears once the buyer pays the premium.","Yes; a commitment itself operates as a lender's satisfaction of the recorded mortgage."],"A commitment states conditions and proposed exceptions. Listing a release as a requirement does not demonstrate that the release has been obtained.");
q(7,9,"44.3","Schedule exception","The proposed title policy expressly excepts a recorded utility easement from coverage. What is the buyer being told about that exception?",["The specified easement is outside the proposed coverage.","The easement will automatically be released when the buyer accepts the proposed policy.","The insurer is guaranteeing relocation of the utility as a condition of charging the premium.","The easement cannot burden the land because only defects omitted from a commitment are valid."],"An exception limits the policy’s coverage. It is not a release or a promise to remove the excepted interest.");
q(7,9,"43.2","Inquiry from possession","A buyer views a building that a third party visibly occupies even though the seller describes it as vacant. Which response addresses the inconsistency before closing?",["Investigate the occupant's rights and possession information.","Accept the vacant-property description because a seller's statement overrides visible occupancy.","Assume the occupant is a trespasser whenever no lease appears in the seller's listing documents.","Promise immediate vacancy because recording the buyer's deed always cancels every occupant's rights."],"Visible possession can call for inquiry into rights not explained by the seller’s account. The actual possession and transaction documents need to be reconciled.");
q(7,9,"40.2","Entity signing authority","The record owner is an LLC, but an individual member proposes to sign the conveyance without evidence of authority. Which question must the title and closing review resolve?",["The proposed signer's authority to act for the LLC.","Only whether the signer is an LLC member, because every member necessarily has unrestricted signing authority.","Only the signer's ownership percentage, because the largest member always may convey independently.","Whether the signer discussed the listing price, which itself establishes authority to convey."],"Entity ownership makes signing authority a distinct issue. Membership or a name on marketing material alone is not proof of authority to execute the conveyance.");
q(7,9,'41.1','Stated warranty scope','A deed expressly limits the grantor’s title warranty to defects arising through the grantor, rather than defects arising before the grantor owned the property. Which warranty scope does the question describe?', ['A special or limited warranty.','A warranty against every defect arising at any time.','A promise that every appliance will work for one year.','A title insurer’s coverage of every future zoning change.'], 'The stated warranty is limited to the grantor’s period or acts, unlike a general warranty. The deed’s actual terms and applicable law must be reviewed in a real conveyance.');
q(7,9,"44.4","Future events and title coverage","A buyer assumes an owner’s title policy necessarily covers a zoning change enacted several years after the policy date. Which response correctly distinguishes title coverage from that assumption?",["Check the policy; future zoning changes are not automatically insured.","Assume every change affecting property value is covered unless it had been announced before closing.","Treat payment of the premium as permanently preserving the zoning classification at the policy date.","Ask the insurer to enforce the previous ordinance because a policy automatically binds the municipality."],"Title coverage is defined by the policy and its date, exceptions, and exclusions. It does not automatically insure every future event affecting use or value.");
q(7,9,"42.2","Estate authority and sale timing","The listed owner has died, and a relative asks to sign a deed as seller solely because the relative expects to inherit. What should be established before relying on that signature?",["Who has the required authority to convey.","Only whether the relative is named as a beneficiary, because that always supplies immediate authority to sign.","Only the deceased owner's old power of attorney, which necessarily continues unchanged after death.","The relative's willingness to promise the buyer clear title without further estate or title review."],"An expectation of inheritance does not itself establish authority to convey for the estate. Counsel and title review should resolve the actual succession and authority before scheduling performance.");

// WI-08: finance, topic 10.
q(8,10,'51.1','Loan-to-value','For this exercise the lender uses a $425,000 value and offers an 80% loan-to-value loan. What loan amount results?', ['$340,000.','$345,000.','$85,000.','$531,250.'], '425,000 × 0.80 = $340,000. The stated value basis controls this exercise.');
q(8,10,'45.2','Debt-to-income','A borrower has $7,500 gross monthly income, a proposed $1,850 housing payment, and $550 other monthly debt counted by this exercise. What total debt-to-income ratio results?', ['32%.','24.67%.','40%.','7.33%.'], '(1,850 + 550) / 7,500 = 0.32. This computes the supplied ratio, not a lender’s approval threshold.');
q(8,10,'51.2','Points and payment break-even','Compared with an otherwise identical loan offer, a $312,000 loan charges 1.25 points and reduces the payment by $65 per month. Ignoring taxes, time value, and any other difference, how many months of that saving recover the upfront points?', ['60 months.','48 months.','6 months.','75 months.'], 'Points cost 312,000 × 0.0125 = $3,900. At $65 monthly savings, 3,900 / 65 = 60 months. This simplified comparison does not promise actual loan suitability.');
q(8,10,'51.3','Principal reduction','The opening loan balance is $180,000 at a stated 6% annual rate calculated monthly. A $1,250 principal-and-interest payment is made; ignore fees and escrows. What part of that payment reduces principal?', ['$350.','$900.','$1,250.','$10,800.'], 'Monthly interest is 180,000 × 0.06 / 12 = $900. Principal reduction is 1,250 − 900 = $350.');
q(8,10,'46.1','Promise and collateral','At loan closing the borrower signs a note and a mortgage. Which pairing correctly identifies their principal functions?', ['The note is the repayment promise; the mortgage secures the obligation with the real estate.','The mortgage is the purchase deed; the note changes the zoning.','The note is title insurance; the mortgage is a tax receipt.','Both documents merely describe the borrower’s moving schedule.'], 'Debt and security are distinct. The note evidences the repayment obligation and the mortgage supplies the lien securing that obligation.');
q(8,10,'49.1','Balloon balance','A financing plan requires monthly interest-only payments for five years and then repayment of the entire principal still outstanding. What should a buyer recognize about the final required payment?', ['It is a balloon payment of the outstanding principal, not principal that has already amortized away.','The principal necessarily reaches zero through interest-only payments.','The seller must forgive the principal because five years have elapsed.','An interest-only payment is identical to full amortization.'], 'Interest-only payments do not reduce principal. A stated maturity requiring the balance calls for a balloon payoff or other permitted funding arrangement.');
q(8,10,'48.1','Land-contract interests','Under a stated land contract, the vendor retains legal title until the contract’s payment conditions are fulfilled while the vendee makes installment payments and holds the purchaser’s equitable interest. Which party is the vendor?', ['The seller retaining legal title under the agreement.','The purchaser making the installment payments.','The appraiser estimating market value.','The county recording clerk.'], 'Vendor and vendee identify seller and purchaser. The question expressly states the land-contract structure rather than assuming it conveys a deed at the first payment.');
q(8,10,'50.3','Loan servicing','After a loan is originated, another company begins collecting the scheduled payments and handling the borrower’s escrow account. What function is that company performing?', ['Servicing the loan.','Re-surveying the collateral boundary.','Issuing the property’s building permit.','Creating the seller’s listing agreement.'], 'Collecting payments and administering the loan account are servicing functions; origination, ownership of the loan, and servicing are separate concepts.');
q(8,10,'51.5','Express proration convention','The contract for this exercise specifies a 360-day year and charges the seller with 120 days of a $5,400 annual expense. What seller share follows that convention?', ['$1,800.','$1,775.34.','$3,600.','$450.'], '5,400 / 360 × 120 = $1,800. The problem stipulates its convention and does not imply that Wisconsin universally mandates a 360-day year.');
q(8,10,'51.6','Cash-to-close reconciliation','A closing worksheet shows a $390,000 price, a $300,000 loan, $8,500 buyer costs, $12,000 earnest money already credited, and a permitted $4,000 seller credit. Ignoring other adjustments, how much additional buyer cash is required?', ['$82,500.','$98,500.','$86,500.','$94,500.'], '390,000 + 8,500 − 300,000 − 12,000 − 4,000 = $82,500. Earnest money and the stated seller credit reduce the additional cash required.');

// WI-09: valuation, topic 11.
q(9,11,'53.3','Comparable adjustment arithmetic','A comparable sold for $365,000 and has an amenity worth $15,000 more than the subject’s equivalent feature. All other differences are ignored. What is its adjusted indication for the subject?', ['$350,000.','$380,000.','$365,000.','$15,000.'], 'A superior comparable is adjusted downward toward the subject: 365,000 − 15,000 = $350,000.');
q(9,11,'54.1','Cost approach','The exercise supplies $95,000 land value, $310,000 replacement cost of improvements, and $45,000 accrued depreciation. What cost-approach indication follows?', ['$360,000.','$405,000.','$450,000.','$170,000.'], 'Land plus improvement cost less depreciation is 95,000 + 310,000 − 45,000 = $360,000.');
q(9,11,'54.2','Direct capitalization','A property’s stated annual net operating income is $54,000. Using the exercise’s 9% capitalization rate, what value indication results?', ['$600,000.','$486,000.','$4,860.','$486,000,000.'], 'Value = net operating income / capitalization rate = 54,000 / 0.09 = $600,000. The rate is a scenario input, not a promised market rate.');
q(9,11,'54.1','Functional obsolescence','A structurally sound office floor has a layout that requires employees to pass through one tenant’s private workroom to reach another tenant’s space. The market discounts this inefficient design. Which category best describes the stated loss?', ['Functional obsolescence.','Physical deterioration caused only by worn roof shingles.','An increase in land area.','A mathematical mortgage amortization error.'], 'An inadequate or inefficient design is functional obsolescence. The scenario distinguishes it from physical wear and from an external influence.');
q(9,11,"52.3","Highest-and-best-use screening","A proposed hotel would produce the largest projected income, but the project is not legally permissible and there is no established path to the required permission. Why does that proposal not establish present highest and best use?",["Profitability alone fails the legal-permissibility test.","The projected income controls automatically, because highest and best use is exclusively an income comparison.","Physical feasibility removes the need to evaluate whether the proposed use is legally permissible.","The owner's stated preference establishes highest and best use even when the required permission is absent."],"Highest and best use requires legal permissibility, physical possibility, financial feasibility, and maximum productivity. A hypothetical profit does not cure a failed legal-permissibility test.");
q(9,11,"53.4","Reconciling evidence","One recent comparable closely matches the subject; two older sales require large, uncertain adjustments. What is a reasoned reconciliation?",["Weight the more relevant, reliable evidence appropriately.","Average every sale equally because the number of required adjustments cannot affect reliability.","Choose the highest indication because reconciliation is intended to maximize the seller's asking price.","Ignore the recent comparable because fewer adjustments provide less information than older, dissimilar sales."],"Reconciliation evaluates relevance and reliability. A numerical average alone does not account for uncertain adjustments or differing comparability.");

// WI-10: fair housing (12), conduct (13), consumer protection (14).
q(10,12,'57.1','Steering based on religion','A buyer specifies price, transit access, and three bedrooms. The agent instead selects neighborhoods according to where people of the buyer’s religion supposedly belong. What is the problem?', ['The agent is steering on a protected characteristic rather than applying the buyer’s stated housing criteria.','The agent is performing a required religious-matching service.','There can be no discrimination if the properties have three bedrooms.','The agent may use religion if the advice is not written down.'], 'Unlawful housing discrimination is prohibited in professional real-estate activity. Steering based on religion substitutes a protected characteristic for the consumer’s neutral search criteria.', 'REEB 24.03(1); Wis. Stat. 106.50(1m)(h), (2)');
q(10,12,'57.1','Blockbusting','A salesperson pressures owners to list by claiming that the arrival of residents of a different race will destroy neighborhood values. Which practice is illustrated?', ['Blockbusting through discriminatory fear-based solicitation.','A neutral comparative market analysis using verified sales.','A permitted way to guarantee future prices.','A required seller-condition disclosure.'], 'Inducing sales through racial fear is discriminatory conduct, not evidence-based pricing advice.', 'REEB 24.03(1); Wis. Stat. 106.50(2m)');
q(10,12,'57.5','Discriminatory screening instruction','A landlord instructs the property manager to reject every otherwise qualified applicant because of the applicant’s religion. No exemption is asserted. How should the manager handle the instruction?', ['Refuse the discriminatory screening instruction and use lawful criteria.','Follow it because the landlord is the client.','Follow it if the rejected applicants are given a different explanation.','Use it only when applications arrive by telephone.'], 'The professional duty against unlawful discrimination is not overridden by a client’s instruction or by concealing the actual screening reason.', 'REEB 24.03(1); Wis. Stat. 106.50(1m)(h), (2)');
q(10,13,"58.2","Competent assistance","A licensee unfamiliar with a specialized property type proposes to proceed under REEB 24.03(2)(a) by obtaining competent assistance. What must accompany that route?",["Identify the assistant and describe their contribution in the transaction records.","Keep the assistance confidential so the client does not question the original licensee's competence.","Use any colleague's name in the file without checking competence or documenting actual assistance.","Obtain a general client disclaimer instead of assistance, because consent eliminates the competence rule."],"The competent-assistance route requires the assisting person to be identified and their contribution described in the transaction records. It is not a waiver of competence or a substitute for actual qualified assistance.","REEB 24.03(2)(a)");
q(10,13,'58.6','Prohibited compensation arrangement','An owner offers a firm all proceeds above a fixed amount as the firm’s commission, rather than a negotiated fee that is not a net listing. The owner is fully informed and enthusiastic. Under REEB 24.10, can the firm use this net listing?', ['No; net listings are strictly prohibited.','Yes, because the owner suggested it.','Yes, if the surplus is put in the firm’s operating account.','Yes, whenever the property is expensive.'], 'REEB 24.10 is a prohibition, not merely a caution that informed consent can remove.', 'REEB 24.10');
q(10,13,"60.1","Confidential offer terms","A competing buyer asks an agent for another buyer’s exact offer price and financing terms. No right-of-first-refusal exception applies. Which distinction does REEB 24.12 make?",["Keep terms confidential; permitted status facts differ.","Release price and financing whenever a competing buyer promises to improve those terms.","Release every signed offer because a signature automatically places the bargain in the public record.","Protect only the buyer's identity while treating every other term as available to competing buyers."],"The rule separates protected terms from specified status facts that may be disclosed; it does not authorize an informal auction of confidential terms.","REEB 24.12");
q(10,14,"61.2","Prompt later inspection after instructed submission","A buyer specifically instructs a licensee to submit a written offer before the property can be accessed. Access becomes available afterward. Under the current REEB 24.07(1)(c), what follows?",["Inspect promptly afterward if access is provided.","Treat the buyer's instruction to submit first as a permanent waiver of the later inspection requirement.","Refuse every instructed pre-access offer because the current rule contains no exception.","Accept another agent's price opinion instead of inspecting when access later becomes available."],"The current paragraph recognizes lack of access or a party’s instruction to submit first and then requires prompt inspection afterward if access is provided.","REEB 24.07(1)(c), effective 2026-03-01");
q(10,14,"61.2","Material inconsistency with a report","During the required inspection, a licensee observes a condition materially inconsistent with a seller’s statement that a visible retaining wall is undamaged. What does REEB 24.07(6) require concerning the inconsistency?",["Disclose the material inconsistency timely in writing to all parties.","Retain the observation privately because a seller's written statement always permits unconditional reliance.","Wait until a party asks specifically whether the earlier statement matches the agent's observation.","Disclose only orally to the firm's client and assume that the client will relay the information."],"Reliance on seller or third-party statements does not authorize ignoring materially inconsistent observations; paragraph (6) specifically requires written disclosure of that inconsistency.","REEB 24.07(6)");
q(10,14,'61.3','Limits of accessible inspection','An accessible-property inspection reveals no safe access to a sealed crawl space, and opening it would require removing a locked panel and entering a hazardous area. Which statement matches REEB 24.07(1)(d)?', ['The rule does not require the licensee to undertake those invasive or hazardous inspection steps.','The licensee must enter regardless of danger because an inspection is required.','A licensee must certify that the unseen crawl space has no defect.','The seller’s refusal of hazardous entry proves the property is defect-free.'], 'The required licensee inspection is not a technical building inspection; the rule excludes specified invasive, obstructed, and hazardous activities. Limits on inspection do not justify unsupported assurances.', 'REEB 24.07(1)(d)');
q(10,14,"61.3","Observation is not a diagnosis","A licensee sees an unfamiliar stain and lacks competent evidence identifying its cause. What response respects both reasonable care and limits of expertise?",["Describe observations accurately and seek competent investigation.","Certify the building safe because uncertain evidence may never be relevant to reasonable care.","Certify a structural defect because every unfamiliar stain conclusively establishes material damage.","Wait for closing before evaluating later reliable evidence that contradicts the seller's report."],"Current duties of reasonable skill and care, competence, and disclosure remain operative. An unexplained observation is not permission to invent a technical conclusion or to ignore information that establishes a disclosure duty.","Wis. Stat. 452.133(1)(b), (c); REEB 24.03(2), 24.07(2), (6)");

// WI-11: leases (15), management (16).
q(11,15,'63.1','Fixed lease term','A lease expressly begins November 1 and ends the following October 31, with no renewal assumed in the question. Which leasehold classification matches this definite term?', ['An estate for years.','A tenancy at sufferance from its first day.','A fee-simple estate conveyed to the tenant.','An easement appurtenant.'], 'An estate for years is a tenancy for a definite term; it need not last multiple years despite the name.');
q(11,15,'64.1','Sublease retaining a reversion','A tenant transfers occupancy for four months of the eight months remaining on the tenant’s lease and retains the last four months. Which classification fits the retained portion of the term?', ['A sublease.','An assignment of the entire remaining term with no retained reversion.','A conveyance of the landlord’s fee title.','A mortgage foreclosure.'], 'Retaining part of the remaining leasehold term distinguishes the stated sublease from transferring the entire remaining term.');
q(11,15,"65.2","Pre-tenancy repair promise","Before entering a residential rental agreement, the landlord promises to replace a broken bedroom window by a stated date. What does ATCP 134.07 require about a repair promise made before the initial agreement?",["Put the promise and completion date or period in writing.","State the promise orally, because the written rental agreement automatically includes all prior conversations.","Promise repair without a date because the rule gives every landlord unlimited discretion over timing.","Treat the initial rent payment as the tenant's waiver of every uncompleted pre-tenancy repair promise."],"ATCP 134.07 requires pre-initial-agreement repair promises to be written and to specify the completion date or time period. The actual rule also addresses delays beyond the landlord’s control.","ATCP 134.07");
q(11,15,"65.2","No self-help eviction","A Wisconsin residential tenant is behind on rent. The landlord asks the manager to remove the door and shut off service to force the tenant out instead of using a lawful eviction process. What is the correct response?",["Refuse self-help and use the applicable lawful process.","Use lockout if the amount owed is documented, because nonpayment itself authorizes physical exclusion.","Remove the door temporarily and label it maintenance so that eviction rules do not apply.","Shut off essential service first and restore it only if the tenant agrees to surrender possession."],"ATCP 134.09 prohibits self-help eviction; a tenant cannot be excluded through the proposed tactics instead of judicial eviction proceedings as required.","ATCP 134.09(7)");
q(11,16,'66.4','Vacancy and operating income','A management forecast has $144,000 potential rent, a $12,000 vacancy and collection allowance, $6,000 other operating income, and $51,000 operating expenses. Debt service and capital spending are excluded. What projected net operating income results?', ['$87,000.','$99,000.','$81,000.','$138,000.'], 'Effective operating income is 144,000 − 12,000 + 6,000 = $138,000; subtracting $51,000 expenses gives $87,000. Ignoring the vacancy allowance overstates the result.');

// WI-12: land use (17), environment (18).
q(12,17,'68.3','Public permission and private restrictions','A municipal permit allows a proposed detached workshop, but a recorded covenant separately restricts outbuildings. Does the permit by itself remove the private restriction?', ['No; public authorization and the private covenant are separate controls that both need evaluation.','Yes; every building permit deletes all recorded covenants.','Yes, if the owner has already ordered construction materials.','No; therefore the zoning permit must necessarily be invalid.'], 'Public approval does not itself release a private covenant. The controls coexist, and their actual operation requires review rather than assuming either cancels the other.');
q(12,17,'67.4','What a permit proves','A seller supplies a building permit for an addition, and the buyer concludes that every component of the entire house is guaranteed free of defects. Which explanation is sound?', ['A permit and code process are not an unlimited condition guarantee or a substitute for the buyer’s appropriate inspection.','A permit insures every appliance against future failure.','Permit issuance proves no work was performed beyond the permit’s scope.','A permit replaces every other transaction disclosure.'], 'Permits concern authorized work and code administration; they do not guarantee every condition, scope detail, or future performance of the whole property.');
q(12,18,"69.2","Environmental conclusion from an untested material","A warehouse contains an old insulating material that the agent cannot reliably identify. A buyer asks the agent to certify it asbestos-free. Which response is appropriate?",["Refer identification and risk assessment to a qualified professional.","Certify the material safe if its surface is intact because visual appearance conclusively proves composition.","Declare it asbestos solely because the building is old, without identifying the material or testing basis.","Assure the buyer that painting the material automatically eliminates every regulatory or health concern."],"Recognition of an issue and competent investigation differ from a technical environmental determination. A licensee should not give an unsupported safety or hazard certification.","REEB 24.03(2)(a), (d)");
q(12,18,"69.2","Environmental report distribution","A qualified third party supplies a written environmental-condition report. A licensee wants to rely on it under REEB 24.07(5). What must the licensee also do under that provision?",["Give copies timely to all parties.","Give the report only to the firm's client, because all-party delivery would waive the right to rely on it.","Delay delivery until contingencies expire so that the report cannot affect the buyer's decision.","Replace the report with an oral summary and discard the third party's written qualifications and findings."],"The rule permits reliance on a qualified third party’s written report if copies are provided timely to all parties. Separate duties still address materially inconsistent information.","REEB 24.07(5), (6)");

// WI-13: miscellaneous laws, topic 19.
q(13,19,"71.6","Prompt submission to the firm","A salesperson receives a buyer’s earnest-money check payable for handling by the associated firm. The salesperson considers keeping it in a personal briefcase for several days while waiting for a more convenient trip to the office. What does REEB 18.031(2) require?",["Submit the trust funds to the firm promptly.","Hold the check until the commission is earned because its payable-to-firm designation makes it firm income.","Delay submission until the salesperson next visits the office, regardless of the elapsed time.","Deposit it into a personal account temporarily if a separate spreadsheet records the owner's balance."],"The salesperson must promptly submit trust funds to the firm. Personal convenience is not a substitute for the statutory handling process.","REEB 18.031(2)");
q(13,19,'70.2','Record-retention floor and exceptions','A Wisconsin transaction closed on June 10, 2026. No longer federal requirement or active Board investigation applies. Under REEB 15.04(1), through what date must its transaction records be retained at a minimum?', ['June 10, 2028.','June 10, 2027.','December 31, 2026.','Only until the commission is paid.'], 'The rule’s minimum is two years from closing for a consummated transaction, subject to the stated longer-retention conditions. 2026 plus two years is 2028.', 'REEB 15.04(1)');
q(13,19,'70.5','Address-change notification','A Wisconsin real-estate licensee changes the address on the license record. Under REEB 23.02, what notification deadline applies?', ['Notify the Department in writing within 30 days of the change.','Wait until the next transaction closes.','Notify only if a client complains.','Wait five years and include the change with a trust-account audit.'], 'REEB 23.02 requires written notification within 30 days for the specified name or address change; the rule is not a continuing-education provision.', 'REEB 23.02');
q(13,19,'71.3','Interest on client funds','The firm holds client funds in the common interest-bearing trust account described in REEB 18.031(3)(a). After permitted service charges, who is the beneficial owner of the interest under that provision?', ['The Wisconsin Department of Administration, for the program described by the rule.','The firm, simply because its name appears on the account.','The salesperson who received the check.','The seller in every transaction regardless of the type of funds.'], 'REEB 18.031(3)(a) specifies Department of Administration beneficial ownership of interest from the described client-funds account. Other trust-fund interest arrangements have their own conditions; the firm cannot assume the interest is its revenue.', 'REEB 18.031(3)(a)');

